    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d348">

                <pb facs="0494" n="390" xml:id="pg390"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 837.77/86.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the British Ambassador.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-12-09">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 9, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: I have the honor to
                    acknowledge the receipt of your note of November 27th, enclosing a letter to you
                    from Sir William Lawrence Young, the Chairman of the Cuban Central Railway
                    Company, concerning the complaint of his company against the action recently
                    taken by the Cuban Government in the matter of the North Coast Railway
                    concession. I have the pleasure of informing you that the substance of this
                    letter, as well as of the enclosures of Lord E. Percey’s note of November 30th
                    and of Mr. Mitchell Innes’s note left with Mr. Clark on the 2d instant, has been
                    communicated by telegraph to the American Minister at Habana with instructions
                    to investigate and report on the matter.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">P. C. Knox</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
